As [documented](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/contributing/development/core_and_modules/common_engine_methods_and_macros.html#microbenchmarking), a crude solution can be implemented as follows:

```cpp
uint64_t begin = OS::get_singleton()->get_ticks_usec();

// Your code here...

uint64_t end = OS::get_singleton()->get_ticks_usec();
print_line(vformat("Snippet took %d microseconds", end - begin));
```

This has a few downsides compared to the proposed macros:

- Requires more typing to benchmark a portion of code, or creating an IDE-specific snippet. By contrast, macros can be autocompleted and require typing less characters. The goal is to reduce the required effort to benchmark code as much as possible, so that people don't forget to benchmark code when it's important to do so :slightly_smiling_face: 
- No display of minimum/maximum/average run time, as well as the number of iterations run since the engine started.
- No color coding of the iteration's run time (which helps visually detect outliers).
